<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>(a) Write an equation for the locus of points <m>{6}</m> units from <m>C({-5}, {6})</m>.</p><div><fillin characters="22" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) The locus of a point <m>P</m> is <m>{(x-3)^2+(y+2)^2=45}</m>. Give the centre and the radius of the circle, the radius correct to 1 decimal place where it is not a whole number.</p><div>Centre <m>\big(</m><fillin characters="4" name="ans2"/>, <fillin characters="4" name="ans3"/><m>\big)</m>   </div><div>Radius <fillin characters="6" name="ans4"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) By Pythagoras, the distance from <m>P(x, y)</m> to <m>C({-5}, {6})</m> is <m>\sqrt{(x - a)^2 + (y - b)^2}</m> with <m>a = {-5}</m> and <m>b = {6}</m>. Setting it equal to <m>{6}</m> and squaring both sides gives</p><p><me>{(x+5)^2+(y-6)^2=36}</me></p><p>Any equivalent equation is accepted, for example the expanded form.</p><p>(b) In <m>(x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2</m> the centre is <m>(h, k)</m> and the right-hand side is the square of the radius. So the centre is <m>({3}, {-2})</m> and the radius is <m>\sqrt{{45}} \approx {6.7}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>{\left(y-6\right)}^2+{\left(x+5\right)}^2=36</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>3</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>-2</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>6.7082039325</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>